WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.060
For this exercise, we're going to short
row shape a single side of a neckline.

2
00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:08.660
This would be as if you were
knitting the front of a cardigan.

3
00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:13.880
For the demo, I've added a simple stockinette band, but the focus is following

4
00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:16.700
the pattern and the
shaping of the neckline.

5
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.380
Using short rows. Here is my pattern.

6
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.580
I'll start by casting on 28
stitches and knit for 24 rows.

7
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.780
I'm using a manual wrap method

8
00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:34.580
for the short rows, so I'll be putting
needles into hold opposite the carriage.

9
00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:39.020
My first instruction is
to bind off 6 stitches.

10
00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:40.340
Instead of binding off,

11
00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:45.300
I'm going to hold these 6 needles.
With my carriage and hold,

12
00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:49.580
I'll knit across, wrap, and knit back.

13
00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:53.360
Remember, the wrap prevents holes.

14
00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.820
The next instruction is to bind
off or hold three stitches.

15
00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.640
Knit, wrap, knit,

16
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.460
bind off two stitches,knit, wrap, knit.

17
00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:15.620
And then we'll bind off one stitch
every other row five times.

18
00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.100
Don't forget to wrap.

19
00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:23.490
Remember, short row shaping
takes place on every other row.

20
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.780
So if your knitting pattern has you
knitting three rows or five rows or some

21
00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:37.720
other combination, you'll need to adjust
your decreases for every other row.

22
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.020
With the shaping done,

23
00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:46.540
before going any further,
I'm confirming that I have 12 stitches

24
00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:51.240
in work and these will
be my shoulder stitches.

25
00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.210
Keeping those shaping stitches in hold,

26
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.060
I'll finish knitting the rest
of the neck opening.

27
00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:03.600
Notice that I'm adding weight
so the stitches knit properly.

28
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.800
Then I'll bind off.

29
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.260
This image isn't an exact replica

30
00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:43.980
of my pattern, but the red dots represent
the stitches in hold and the green dots

31
00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:48.560
represent the stitches
we are about to pick up.

32
00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.920
Picking up stitches to rows, so
the pickup isn't one-to-one.

33
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.580
Plan on three stitches to four rows,

34
00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:06.960
or maybe two stitches to three rows,
depending on your gauge.

35
00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.420
Knit across all the stitches.

36
00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:17.380
I used a contrasting color yarn.
And you have some options here.

37
00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:22.660
You could knit the row with your garment
yarn and simply bind off the edge.

38
00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:27.100
Or you could scrap off,
leaving live stitches.

39
00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:31.820
Once you've knit and joined the back piece
of your sweater, you could then pick up

40
00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:37.120
the bound off or scrapped off neck edges
and finish them with any method you like.

